    "id": "trump-calls-off-livestream-of-fort-hood-88a3d733",
    "title": "Trump calls off livestream of Fort Hood attacker's execution by firing squad",
    "titleJa": "Trump calls off livestream of Fort Hood attacker's execution by firing squad",
    "summaryJa": "Trump said some would \"rather not watch\" the killing of Nidal Hasan, the first military execution in over 60 years.",
    "bodyOriginal": "Trump calls off livestream of Fort Hood attacker's execution by firing squad\n- Published\nUS President Donald Trump has called off plans to livestream the execution of former US army psychiatrist Nidal Hasan by firing squad.\nTrump had previously said he approved of showing the death of the Fort Hood attacker, while Vice-President JD Vance cast doubt on whether it would happen and said he would not watch it.\nTrump's decision to cancel the livestream came after widespread criticism in the US and globally. On Thursday, he said that \"many very good and well meaning people would rather not watch him die\".\n\"I think streaming the execution for all to see might have been a strong deterrent for the World to witness how America deals with Terrorists and SCUM like Hasan,\" Trump said.\n\"He will die, as scheduled, by firing squad, but this event will not be streamed,\" the president added.\nHasan fatally shot 13 unarmed US soldiers at a military base in Texas in 2009, injuring another 32. His execution by firing squad will be the first military execution in more than 60 years.\nTrump said the families of the victims can still view the execution in person if they wish.\n\"They deserve to witness JUSTICE!\" he said.\nAfter the Pentagon announced last week that it would execute Hasan on 3 December 2026 by firing squad and livestream the event, a cascade of criticism quickly followed from human rights groups and many others. The United Nations called the plans \"abhorrent\".\nWhile there were more than 2,700 executions across 17 countries last year, only two nations carried them out publicly. According to Amnesty International, 11 public executions were known to have been carried out in Iran in 2025 and another six in Afghanistan.\nUS Defence Secretary Pete Hegseth defended his decision to air Hasan's execution online, saying that \"people need to understand that there's serious consequences for these types of things\".\nBut as criticism mounted, Trump appeared to hedge his support, saying on Friday that he \"absolutely\" approved of the livestream plan, but adding that the Pentagon was still \"making a determination\" on it.\nThe president also called Hasan \"totally nuts\".\n\"I really don't care how he goes, as long as he goes, and I think that the death penalty is a very worthy way of going for him,\" he said.\nJohn Galligan, a lawyer for Hasan working primarily on civil issues, told the BBC that Trump's decision to cancel the livestream of his execution \"comes as no surprise\".\n\"It was initially a dumb decision and statement by Hegseth to livestream any execution - and it violated the Army's own regulation,\" Galligan said.\nHe said Hasan is paralysed from the chest down, something that Trump \"allegedly didn't even know\" when he approved the livestream plan.\n\"The damage has already been done - the Army is now just trying to clean up the mess,\" Galligan said. \"It will permanently remain a stain on our military justice system.\"\nHe added that it was not clear if Hasan was aware yet of Trump's \"reversal\" of the livestream decision.\nHasan opened fire in a crowded medical building where deploying soldiers were awaiting check-ups and vaccines.\nHe later admitted in court that he was the gunman and apologised for \"any mistakes\". He said during his court-martial in 2013 that he believed he was protecting Muslims and the Taliban in Afghanistan from the US military.\nThe killings on 5 November 2009 were the deadliest ever non-combat attack on an American military base.\nUS military law requires that a military execution be approved by the US president. It also requires appellate review, when a higher court inspects the decision and gives a final judgement on its legality to move forward.\n- Published5 days ago\n- Published5 days ago",
